---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'About', href: '/about' },
  { label: 'Corrections', href: '/about/corrections' }
];

const article = {
  publishDate: "2026-07-04",
  modifiedDate: "2026-07-16",
  section: "Editorial Standards"
};

// Material corrections log. Each entry documents what was wrong, what
// is correct, and how to verify. The ledger is reverse-chronological.
// Adding an entry requires publishing an updated article AND a small
// site-health stat bump in the nav (a single counter — handled
// elsewhere; this page is the canonical source).
const corrections = [
  {
    date: "2026-07-16",
    slug: "maine-ocp-testing-rule-watch-2026",
    title: "Testing and edibles guides — proposed 2026 OCP rulemaking separated from current requirements",
    what_was_wrong:
      "Several guides described a universal full testing panel for every adult-use product or edible and treated destruction as the automatic outcome of any failed test. The regulations guide also referred to unspecified 2026 testing updates as already effective. Those summaries did not account for product matrix, final form, prior input testing, or Chapter 40's analyte-specific retest and remediation pathways.",
    what_is_correct:
      "OCP's July 2026 Chapter 5 and Chapter 40 documents are proposals, not effective rules. The draft Chapter 40 would clarify final-form testing, provide an edible contaminant-testing exception when the concentrate input has passed all mandatory testing, and specify that two passing potency-retest samples use the higher result for labeling. Current compliance still follows the final Chapter 40 rule effective November 6, 2024 until OCP adopts replacement text and publishes an effective date. Testing and failed-batch disposition depend on the product matrix, input-testing history, failed analyte, and applicable retest or remediation pathway.",
    verified_via:
      "Primary: maine.gov/dafs/ocp/adult-use/rules-statutes/rulemaking/proposed-rules; OCP's 18-691 CMR Chapter 5 and Chapter 40 public-comment drafts; current final 18-691 CMR Chapter 40 (effective November 6, 2024). OCP public comment closes August 14, 2026 at 5:00 p.m.; hearing August 3, 2026 at 10:00 a.m.",
    published_in: [
      { name: "Cannabis edibles compliance guide", href: "/guides/maine-cannabis-edibles-compliance" },
      { name: "Cannabis product testing guide", href: "/guides/maine-cannabis-product-testing-guide" },
      { name: "How to read a Maine cannabis COA", href: "/guides/cannabis-coa-maine-how-to-read" },
      { name: "How to read a Maine cannabis COA for terpenes", href: "/blog/how-to-read-maine-cannabis-coa-terpenes-2026" },
      { name: "Cannabis cultivation guide", href: "/guides/maine-cannabis-cultivation-guide" },
      { name: "Cannabis waste management guide", href: "/guides/maine-cannabis-waste-management" },
      { name: "Maine cannabis regulations guide", href: "/guides/maine-cannabis-regulations" },
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-07-05",
    slug: "maine-edible-dose-cap",
    title: "Maine edible dose cap — 5/100 corrected to 10/200",
    what_was_wrong:
      "Multiple Maine Dispensary Guide pages described the Maine adult-use cannabis edible dose cap as '5mg per serving / 100mg per package' and cited a 'medical program 250mg per package' ceiling. These claims were based on the pre-2023 statutory text of Title 28-B §703(1)(F).",
    what_is_correct:
      "Title 28-B §703(1)(F), as amended by PL 2023, c. 396, §19 (effective 2023), sets the Maine adult-use edible cannabis cap at 10mg THC per single serving and 200mg THC per package, with a 10% allowable variance (floor 0.6 mg, ceiling 5 mg). The Maine medical cannabis program (Title 22 ch. 558-C; 18-691 CMR ch. 2) is provider-discretion and does not impose the same statutory per-package edible cap as the adult-use program; the practical medical ceiling is operator-determined and documented on each product's COA.",
