          </div>
        )}
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="methodology">
    <h2>How we handle corrections</h2>
    <p>Editorial corrections are a leading indicator of editorial integrity. Here is how our process works:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Detection.</strong> Corrections come from primary-source research, internal re-audits, operator feedback, and reader reports. AI-assisted spotting is used as a starting point but every entry on this page is reviewed by a human editor before publication.</li>
      <li><strong>Severity.</strong> Every claim touching YMYL (regulatory, financial, operator data) is checked against statute, the operator's own current site, the relevant municipal office, and primary news sources. Material errors are corrected, not papered over.</li>
      <li><strong>Action.</strong> Each correction updates the affected guide with a primary-source citation, modifies the article date, and pushes an entry into this log on the same commit.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-review.</strong> After a correction, we add a quarterly reviewer spot-check on the topic to catch any reintroduced error.</li>
      <li><strong>No fabrication.</strong> When a house number is unknown or a statute citation is unverified, we say so explicitly in the article — we do not invent a plausible-looking value.</li>
      <li><strong>Reader reports.</strong> Spot an error? Email <a href="mailto:corrections@mainedispensaryguide.com">corrections@mainedispensaryguide.com</a>. Confirmed reports are corrected within five business days; nothing about the reporter is published without consent.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--color-text-light);">Last updated: {today}. This page is the canonical source for corrections published on mainedispensaryguide.com.</p>
  </section>
